Found the mechanism. [audio.c:1267-1277](../../nuttx/audio/audio.c#L1267-L1277):

```c
case AUDIOIOC_GETBUFFERINFO:
  ret = lower->ops->ioctl(lower, AUDIOIOC_GETBUFFERINFO, arg);
  if (ret >= 0)
    {
      upper->nbuffers = ((struct ap_buffer_info_s *)arg)->nbuffers;
    }
```

`upper->nbuffers` is set **only as a side effect of a successful GETBUFFERINFO**. And [audio.c:786-789](../../nuttx/audio/audio.c#L786-L789) gates allocation on it:

```c
if (upper->periods >= upper->nbuffers)
  {
    return 0;          /* not an error code -- literally zero */
  }
```

So `nbuffers == 0` makes the very first ALLOCBUFFER return 0. That is exactly what I saw, and it's why the return value was such a strange one.

